# 🚀 NeuroGrid True Offline BLE Mesh Implementation

## 🎯 The Objective
Enable true offline peer-to-peer mesh networking for the NeuroGrid Android app, allowing offline devices (no Wi-Fi, no mobile data) to securely broadcast SOS signals and discover each other using physical Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) hardware, while seamlessly syncing with online Gateway devices.

## 🛠️ The Architecture & The Blockers
Capacitor plugins like `@capacitor-community/bluetooth-le` perfectly handle **Scanning (Central Role)**, but Android restricts **Broadcasting (Peripheral Role)** to native OS-level APIs. Javascript cannot emit custom BLE manufacturer payloads natively.

We designed a custom [NeuroMeshPlugin.java](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/android/app/src/main/java/com/neurogrid/app/NeuroMeshPlugin.java) Android Native Bridge to expose `BluetoothLeAdvertiser` to Javascript. However, we hit several deeply technical walls:

### 1. The Capacitor Bridge Race Condition
**The Bug:** The JS layer reported `"NeuroMesh" plugin is not implemented on android`. 
**The Cause:** In [MainActivity.java](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/android/app/src/main/java/com/neurogrid/app/MainActivity.java), the Capacitor bridge is built when `super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)` is called. The custom plugin was being registered *after* the bridge was locked.
**The Fix:** Swapped the order to register [NeuroMeshPlugin](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/android/app/src/main/java/com/neurogrid/app/NeuroMeshPlugin.java#26-188) *before* the `super` call, successfully mapping the Java methods to the Javascript namespace.

### 2. The Android 12+ Permission Void
**The Bug:** The Bluetooth API silently failed despite JS confirming everything.
**The Cause:** Android 12 introduced explicit `BLUETOOTH_ADVERTISE` and `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT` permissions. The Capacitor plugin did not handle these new Peripheral-specific manifests.
**The Fix:** Updated [NeuroMeshPlugin.java](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/android/app/src/main/java/com/neurogrid/app/NeuroMeshPlugin.java) to implement `@CapacitorPlugin(permissions = {...})` and added a `requestPermissions()` hook that [NativeMeshManager.js](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/src/core/mesh/NativeMeshManager.js) calls immediately upon initialization.

### 3. The Object vs. Map Trap
**The Bug:** Devices were advertising, but the scanner couldn't read the data. 
**The Cause:** The Javascript scanner tried to parse the 16-byte payload using an ES6 Map: `result.manufacturerData.get(0xFFFF)`. But Capacitor bridges Java HashMaps as plain JSON objects.
**The Fix:** Changed the code to use string bracket notation: `result.manufacturerData['65535']` to successfully extract the binary array.

### 4. The "Error Code 2" Hardware Overload (Too Many Advertisers)
**The Bug:** The JS layer triggered `BLE Advertising failed to start. Error code: 2`.
**The Cause:** BLE broadcasting is handled continuously by the OS. The React frontend, designed for simulated WebRTC routing, was calling [sendSOS()](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/src/hooks/useMesh.js#98-99) every 5 seconds. This repeatedly commanded the Android BLE Chip to launch a *new* advertising instance until the hardware ran out of memory slots.
**The Fix:** Built a mathematical **Debouncer** in [NativeMeshManager.js](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/src/core/mesh/NativeMeshManager.js). The JS layer checks if the incoming payload (Latitude, Longitude, Priority) has materially changed. If the data is identical to the active broadcast, it blocks the React loop and allows the Android OS to flawlessly continue broadcasting the previous payload in the background.

### 5. The "0 Peers" UI Desync
**The Bug:** Offline devices successfully transmitted data to the Gateway, but their own screen said `0 peers`.
**The Cause:** [useMesh](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/src/hooks/useMesh.js#12-103) React Hook initialized with `0 peers` and only listened for future `peer_joined` events. The background Native Scanner found the Gateway during initial boot (before React mounted), so the UI missed the event.
**The Fix:** Instructed [useMesh](file:///C:/Users/vijay/OneDrive/Desktop/NeuroGrid/client/src/hooks/useMesh.js#12-103) to dynamically initialize reading the underlying `MeshRouter.peerCount` and forced a sync upon component mount.

## 💣 Fighting Windows & Android Studio
Throughout the development cycle, the build constantly failed with the infamous Windows file lock error:
`java.io.IOException: Unable to delete directory ... packageDebugResources` AND `Cannot snapshot ... not a regular file`

Because Windows aggressively locks files being written by processes, the `java.exe` Gradle daemons running in the background of Android Studio would lock corrupted intermediary snapshot files (`ic_launcher.png`, `mergeDebugResources.json`), permanently bricking the build.

**The Solution:**
We deployed aggressive terminal commands to forcefully terminate the daemons and obliterate the cached build artifacts folder entirely, forcing a clean compile:
```powershell
taskkill /F /IM studio64.exe /T
taskkill /F /IM java.exe /T
cmd /c "rmdir /s /q C:\Users\vijay\OneDrive\Desktop\NeuroGrid\client\android\app\build"
```

## ✅ Final Result
The system is now fully operational. Offline devices in the same room automatically connect via BLE, compress their JSON distress models into tiny 16-byte hashes, bounce them peer-to-peer, and hit the online Gateway device. The Gateway then uploads the entire batch to the Rescue Operations Dashboard mapping engine instantly.
